2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.rootsweb.com/~nwa/jeanne.html>.
2 CONT
2 CONT JEANNE LEMARCHAND -- LENEUF DU HERRISON
2 CONT Text by Gary Edwards
2 CONT
2 CONT My Great 11 Grandmother, Jeanne LeMarchand de La Celloniere et La Rocque arrived in New France in 1636 with her sons, Michel and Jacques LeNeuf. The Le Neuf family was the first noble family to settle permanently in New France. He
2 CONC r eldest son, Michel became the Sieur du Herrisson and a Judge; the younger, Jacques was Sieur de La Potterie and was the Governor of Trois Riveres. The LeNeufs, along with their LeGardeur de Repentigny in-laws, were the most illu
2 CONC strious family in the French colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne was married in St. Thury-Harcourt, Caen, Normandy, France by contract on December 1599 to Mathieu LeNeuf du Herrisson. The contract shows her parents as being Lt. Gervais Le Marchand, Sieur de La Celloniere et La Rocque and
2 CONC  Venote de St-Germain.
2 CONT
2 CONT The LeNeufs du Herrisson were given the opportunity in the 1630s to revitalize their dwindling fortunes (the seigneurie of Herrisson was not as prosperous as it was before the bourgeoise). They would be sent to New France to estab
2 CONC lish themselves (and the feudal aristocracy) in the name of the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT The LeNeuf brothers were very fair (the judgements passed down by Michel were very liberal) but rather hot-tempered. One day, Michel and Jacques were fighting with the habitants of Trois-Riveres. Jeanne, hearing the commotion, ste
2 CONC pped out of the Governor's mansion. She too got into the brawl and was punched in the stomache by Guillaume Isabel (he was not a habitant). Despite her noble upbringing, Jeanne apparently loved a fight.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twenty years later, her daughter-in-law, Marguerite LeNeuf de La Potterie nee LeGardeur de Repentigny was found to have been the leader of the liqour trade with the Indians. The Le Neuf family was very much involved in the liquor 
2 CONC and fur trade, both of which made them fabulously wealthy by 17th century standards.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.sylvestre.qc.ca/0000341.htm> with a translated version as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT MATHIEU LENEUF (-1622)
2 CONT Mathieu was born to the vicinity of 1581.
2 CONT Residence place: Caen in Normandie
2 CONT Spouse: Jeanne Le Marchand
2 CONT
2 CONT June 12 1636, a boat coming from France and ordered by Mr. Courpon entered into Quebec roads.  It carried the new governor, Mr. Montmagny, successor of Mr. Champlain who had died December 25 1635.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the same boat, a family coming from Caen arrived.  It was the family Leneuf that was composed of: mother, born Jeanne LeMarchand, widow of Mathieu Leneuf, who died in 1622 and of their four children
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN OF MATHIEU LENEUF AND OF JEANNE LEMARCHAND
2 CONT
2 CONT MICHEL, Sieur of the Hérisson, 35 years old, with his girl Anne, 4 years old.  One does not speak of his spouse.  She had to be dead.  This small Anne Leneuf will marry in 1647, Antoine Desrosiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT JACQUES, Sieur of the Potherie, 30 years old, with his spouse, Marguerite deGardeur of Repentigny and their granddaughter Marie.  They had a son, Michel, Sieur of the Vallières that maried Marie Denys.  Their daughter, Marie, marr
2 CONC ied in 1652, Rene Robineau, Sieur of Bécancour and their six sons and a daughter, born March 7 1672.  She became a nun with the Quebec Ursulines under the name of Sister of the Trinité where she died July 6 1748.  He was the gover
2 CONC nor of Three Rivers, July 7 1668 to May 1669.  October 18 1700, the Seigneurie of Kamouraska belonged to Louis Aubert of Forillon aka Chesnaye, spouse of Barbe Leneuf of the Vallières, granddaughter of Jacques Leneuf, daughter of 
2 CONC Michel.  July 20 1713, this seigneurie is given to Henry Hiché that marries 4 days later Marguerite leGardeur.  August 12 1713, Rene Robineau of Bécancour, signs a concession contract to Gabriel Paradis, at Kamouraska.  Reverend F
2 CONC ather Bernadin Leneuf, Récollet born Jacques Leneuf of the Vallière, son of Michel Leneuf and of Marie Denys, at Three Rivers November 17 1672.  He entered with the Récollets, at Quebec in 1696.  In 1701, he was at Rimouski, first
2 CONC  missionary.  Catherine Leneuf, daughter of Jacques, married Pierre Denis of LaRonde of Quebec, owner of la Canardiere.  A Leneuf of their kinship was secretary of the Cardinal Mazarin.  MADELEINE Leneuf with her spouse, John Fran
2 CONC çois Poutrel (In 1660, this Poutrel returned to Lake Superior) and a 6 years old son, Guy.  In 1644, they had a daughter, Madeleine, that became a nun at the Hospital of Quebec under the name of Sister Augustine of St Michel.  She
2 CONC  died in 1689.  Marie Leneuf (and two sons, Michel, Sieur of Lintot and Louis, Sieur of Normanville.  The spouse of Michel was Marie Françoise Denys, daughter of Simon Denys, Sieur  de la Ronde, 24 years old.  December 15 of this 
2 CONC same year, she marries at Three Rivers, Jean Godefroy who came to the country as servant of the Jesuits and first owned land at Three Rivers.  The concession act is dated back to December 3 1633.  (Jacques Hertel was the second ow
2 CONC ner).  Marie Leneuf died in 1678.  All these Leneuf were established to Three Rivers in 1636.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from:  <http://www.themarxfamily.net/roots/574.HTM>.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne aka LE MARCHAND
2 CONT Born: Abt 1577, Conde-Sur-Noireu, Calvados, France
2 CONT Marriage: Mathieu LENEUF HERISSON 5 Dec 1599, Temple Huguenot, Caen, Normandy, France
2 CONT Died: Aft 1647, Trois-Rivieres, Trois-Rivieres, Quebec, Canada, after age 70
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Links
2 CONT Spouses/Children::
2 CONT Mathieu LENEUF HERISSON
2 CONT
2 CONT Michel LENEUF-DU-HERISSON+
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne married Mathieu LENEUF HERISSON on 5 Dec 1599 in Temple Huguenot, Caen, Normandy, France. (Mathieu LENEUF HERISSON was born about 1580 in Caen, Normandy, France 119 and died on 24 Nov 1622 in Caen, Normandy, France 157.)
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from:  <http://www.genealogiequebec.info/testphp/info.php?no=58380> a translated version follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne Merchant 1
2 CONT
2 CONT She is also known under the name of Jeanne LeMarchand 2.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is the daughter of Sieur Gervais the Merchant of Bellonière and de La Roque 3 and Venote Saint-Germain 3.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is perhaps the mother of Sieur Jacques Leneuf of Pottery 1. The marriage contract of Jeanne the Merchant and Sieur Mathieu Leneuf of duHerisson is signed on December 15, 1636 in Quebec, Capitale-Nationale, Quebec, Canada by fr
2 CONC ont Faribaut 1.
2 CONT
2 CONT List of his/her known children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Michel Leneuf 1 (of Sieur Mathieu Leneuf of du `Herisson)
2 CONT + 2. Marie LeNeuf (1612 - 1688) 4 (of Sieur Mathieu Leneuf of du Herisson)
2 CONT + 3. Anne of the Hedgehog (1632 -) 1 (of Sieur Mathieu Leneuf of du Herisson)
2 CONT
