2 CONC elain dit Lafontaine refers to him as "Royal Judge."
2 CONT
2 CONT The archives of the courthouse of Trois-Rivières have preserved for us some of the judgements handed down by Michel Leneuf. In general they are marked by fairness and common sense. These archives also contain an absorbing account 
2 CONC of the numerous lawsuits that took place between the fiery seigneur and his tenant farmers. He was a typical Norman country squire, intelligent and wily, but fond of legal quibbles.
2 CONT
2 CONT The documents provide us with very little information about his private life. When he arrived in New France he was single or a widower, for he brought out with him a little girl of four who still bore the name Anne Du Hérisson and
2 CONC  not Anne Leneuf Du Hérisson. It is possible, as has been claimed, that she was his illegitimate daughter. No precise information is given in this young womans marriage contract with Antoine Desrosiers dated 24 Nov. 1647.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michel Leneufs burial certificate is not recorded in the Roman Catholic registers of Trois-Rivières, which are preserved intact from 1634. We may, however, assume that he died in 1672, for he was replaced in his post as judge by G
2 CONC illes de Boyvinet on 26 October of that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michel had acted as governor of Trois-Rivières in 1668, as a temporary replacement in this office for René Gaultier de Varennes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond Douville
2 CONT
2 CONT AJTR, MSS, Registres des audiences de la cour de juridiction civile et criminelle. APQ, Documents de la prévôté de Trois-Rivières. Recensement de 1667. Papier terrier de la Cie des I.O. (P.-G. Roy). P.-G. Roy, Inv. concessions, I,
2 CONC  II, V. Raymond Douville, "La dictature de la famille Le Neuf," Cahiers des Dix, XX (1955), 61-89; Visages du vieux Trois-Rivières (Trois-Rivières, 1955). Godbout, Les pionniers de la région trifluvienne. Sulte, Mélanges historiqu
2 CONC es (Malchelosse), XI, 7-38; XIV, 65-67; XIX.
2 CONT
2 CONT ? 2000 University of Toronto/Université Laval
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.kateritekakwitha.org/roots/norm4.htm>:
2 CONT
2 CONT Michel LENEUF du HÉRISSON was born at Caen around 1601. He arrived in New France in 1636. He had a natural child, Anne DU HÉRISSON.  He settled at Trois-Rivières, entered civic life, and his brother was the governor of the post.  
2 CONC He acquired much land.  He was in a dispute with the Jesuits for having obtained a title to some land in the seigneurie of Cap-de-la-Madeleine that had been reserved by the missionaires for an Algonquin settlement.  He died before
2 CONC  26 October 1672. (BD p. 263).
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.google.com/search?q=cache:qVz2BoWizvoJ:pages.infinit.net/dickm1/pafg44.htm+%22jean+leneuf%22&hl=en&ie=UTF-8>:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestor - Sieur du Hérisson Michel LENEUF [Parents] [Album] was born in 1601 at Caen of Lisieux, Normandie, France.  He died October 26 1672 at Immaculate Conception of Three Rivers, Quebec.  Michel was married about 1631 at Norm
2 CONC andie, France.  Michel was employed in the trading of  furs and Royal Judge.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: MICHEL LeNEUF
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Sieur DuHérisson
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Two brothers LeNeuf came to establish with their women and children in New France (and with their mother?), about 1636.  Michel LeNeuf, sieur of the Hérisson, and Jacques LeNeuf, sieur of Pottery.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Sons of Mathieu LeNeuf, Sieur of the Hérisson and of Jeanne LeMarchand, that married in December 1599, at the Huguenot temple of Caen, in the current department of the Calvados, in France.  This family may have nobility
2 CONC  origins dating back to Richard LeNeuf to the fourteenth century.  But, it is very difficult to establish the proof of the link.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: The Sieur du Hérisson, Michel, was born about 1601, in Normandie.  Married about 1631, in Normandie, we are unaware of the name of his spouse.  Their daughter Anne LeNeuf, DuHérisson, was born in France in 1632, arrived
2 CONC  with her parents, becomes spouse, November 24 1647, of ancestor Antoine Desrosiers, born in 1619, native of the Town of Renaison, in the district of Roanne, in Auvergne.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: The oldest, Anne LeNeuf DuHérisson, elderly about 79 years, died October 16 1711, while her husband Antoine Desrosiers already deceased since August 8 1691, age of 72 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: THE ANCESTOR Michel LeNeuf, Sieur DuHérisson, died October 26 1672, at the Immaculée-Conception of Three Rivers.  It is possible that Jeanne Marchand, his mother, born about 1580, in Normandie, could have come to New Fr
2 CONC ance and deceased after 1647, at Three Rivers, but all remains to be proven.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Bécancour represents the territorial location of three former seigneuries: The seigneurie of Dutort, of Cournoyer and of Bécancour.  The fief Dutort was, it seems, the first one that was occupied.  This one belonged to 
2 CONC the sieur Michel LeNeuf DuHérisson that he deeded to his nephew Michel Godfroy of Lintot June 19 1650.  It is on their land that the town of Becancour is currently located.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://ourworld.compuserve.com/homepages/MarjChapman/TRDUHERR.HTM>:
2 CONT
2 CONT LeNEUF du HÉRISSON
2 CONT
2 CONT Michel LeNeuf aka duHÉRISSON was born about 1601 in the parish of Trinité-du-Montin, LeHavre, Rouen, Normandy. He died before October 1672 in Trois-Rivières, P.Q.
2 CONT
2 CONT Considered a provincial nobleman, he was a member of the small nobility of Normandy. His known siblings were a brother, Jacques LeNeuf de la Poterie, a squire, and two sisters, Madeleine LeNeuf married to Pouterel and Marie LeNeuf
2 CONC  married to Godefroy de Lintot, also a squire.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was first and foremost a fur merchant. His initial trip to New France was made in 1627. He returned for permanent settlement with his family in 1636 for the purpose of commercial fur trading rather than colonization. Because of
2 CONC  this, he never developed the large land holdings given to him by the King of France. Other noble families, tied through marriage, arrived at the same time--all with the same goals.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1666 census by Talon, lists his age as approx. 65 years old and able to sign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prominent in the Trois Rivieres area, he served as judge and overlord of an estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also a commercial brewer.
2 CONT
2 CONT His only child and daughter, Anne born 1632 in the bourg of Thierry in Caen, Normandy married Antoine DesROSIERS in 1649 who is my direct ancestor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine Desrosiers and Anne duHérisson had eight children. Sons Jean born 1657 and Antoine born 1664, were members of an association created in 1683 between nine men from Champlain for the purpose of fur trading. The composition o
2 CONC f these associates is noteworthy in that it concentrated the power of three influential families in the region: Dandonneau (Huguenot fur-trading merchants), Chorel and Aubuchon. Jean's wife was Françoise Dandonneau. The associatio
2 CONC n dissolved after the summer of 1688 when Jacques Babie, merchant and principal investor, died.
2 CONT
2 CONT All the sons were involved in the business of procuring and selling furs. Son, Michel aka Desilets born 1652 married Thomasse Artault the daughter of an Algonquin and a fur trader, son Jean aka LeFrenière married Françoise Dandonn
2 CONC eau as mentioned above, Antoine aka DuTremble was part of the syndicate mentioned above and received a passport in 1689 to trade in the west. The west, in this case, was probably no further than the Great Lakes. To control the fur
2 CONC  trading and collect appropriate taxes, the French government issued passports (permits), a form of license. They were difficult to acquire since in many years, only 25 were issued. To conduct fur trading or to travel west without
2 CONC  a passport was considered illegal and subject to punishment, most often fines or the confiscation of furs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son, Pierre aka Dargy born around 1667 married Marguerite AUBUCHON whose uncle was active in the trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne LeNeuf duHérisson died in Champlain in October 1711 at the age of 79.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
