1 NOTE Married 15 Dec 1636 Jean-Baptiste Godefroy at Trois-Rivieres, St Maurice, Quebec, Canada; sources: (c. 15-12-1636); family
2 CONT (11 children) listed in Jette pg 509.
2 CONT <http://ourworld.compuserve.com/homepages/MarjChapman/TRgodefr.HTM> (copy filed 29-12) contains information and vitals relative to her spouse, Jean-Baptiste Godefroy.
2 CONT A monument in Quebec City to LOUIS HEBERT has a plaque on the backside listing JEAN-BAPTISTE GODEFROY and his spouse, MARIE LE NEUF, among others,as settlers of Quebec City in 1635.  This info gathered from the internet (copy file
2 CONC d L-16-10 & R-3-14).
2 CONT Add'l info acquired from the net (copy filed 29-12).
2 CONT Family group sheet of #896 from Sue Rood genealogy (copy filed Sue Rood papers).
2 CONT Family group sheet of #899 from Sue Rood genealogy (copy filed Sue Rood papers).
2 CONT Database of Gilles C. Paquette from the internet (copy filed 29-13).
2 CONT 1666 Census of Trois-Rivieres (the first census of Canada) shows her family as follows: Jean Baptiste Godfroy, age 58; Marie Leneuf, age 54; children: Michel Godfroy, age 29 and his wife Perrinne Picotte, age 21; Louis Godefroy, a
2 CONC ge 27; Joseph Godefroy, age 19; Jean Amador Godefroy, age 17; Pierre Godefroy, age 10; Jean Baptiste Godefroy, age 8; Marie Renee Godefroy, age 13 and daughter of Michel (above), Marie Francoise Ursule, age 6 months.  Also listed 
2 CONC are the following domestics: Pierre Chaperon, age 22; Roger de Latouche, age 19; Gilles Durand, age 24; Nicolas Freron, age 17; Jean Rat, age 23; Charles Desnaut (AKA LaPlume), age 18.  Source for this information is filed under c
2 CONC ensuses.
2 CONT <http://www.genealogie.com/cgi-bin/fo/afficher.cgi?i> (copy filed 29-12) shows the following sources: Jette pg 713; "Dictionnaire Genealogique de nos Origines""--Denis Beauregard Vol 1 pg 68.
2 CONT <http://www.sylvestre.qc.ca/0000341.htm> contains information about Mathieu Leneuf and his family and can be found in Mathieu Leneuf's [#896] textfile.
2 CONT <http://www.smartnet.ca/users/roberochon/bisson.htm> (copy filed 47-4).
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.leveillee.net/ancestry/french.htm>.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie LENEUF (bef. 1616 - d. 1688
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie LENEUF arrived in the country with her mother and brothers in 1636. On 15 Dec 1636, she married Jean GODEFROY, the interpreter at Trois-Rivières. She was a midwife in the young colony. She raised eleven children, nine of who
2 CONC m survived. She died at Trois-Rivières on 27 Oct 1688.   Source:  Biographical Dictionary for The Jesuit Missions in Acadia and New France: 1602-1654, Lucien Campeau, S.J., translated by William Lonc, S.J. & George Topp, S.J., sum
2 CONC mer 2001, p. 263.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://ourworld.compuserve.com/homepages/MarjChapman/TRGODEFR.HTM>.
2 CONT
2 CONT GODEFROY
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Baptiste GODEFROY aka Lintot was born around 1608 in the parish of Trinité-du-Mont, Le Havre, Rouen, Normandy. He died in March 1678 in Trois Rivières, P.Q. at 70.
2 CONT
2 CONT An interpreter and fur merchant, he arrived in Canada with his brother, Thomas, in 1626 under the government of Champlain. They stayed with the Hurons from 1629 to 1632, during the occupation of the Kirkes. Thomas Godefroy de Norm
2 CONC anville had an uncommon ability to learn the native languages and was a brilliant and impressive interpreter. He spoke Iroquois, Huron and Algonquin fluently. Devoted to the work of the missionaries, he presided over prayer meetin
2 CONC gs and served in the administration of baptisms. Endowed with remarkable physical strength and courage, he escaped the Iroquois on two occasions. But the day came when their shrewdness got the best of him. In May 1652, the Iroquoi
2 CONC s stalked de Normanville in the villages and killed him in their fashion. Also killed with him was the missionary, Jacques Buteux.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Baptiste Godefroy established himself at Trois-Rivières in 1634. In December 1636, he married Marie LeNEUF duHÉRISSON, the sister of Michel and Jacques LeNeuf who had arrived that June along with another LeNeuf sister, the mo
2 CONC ther LeNeuf (Jeanne leMarchand) and Michel's young child, Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 1666 census of the Trois-Rivières area by Talon, he is listed as a habitant and a squire and is able to sign. His wife Marie LeNeuf is listed as a mid-wife and is able to sign also. At that time, there were 5 sons and a dau
2 CONC ghter living in the household between the ages of 21 to 8.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie LeNeuf duHérisson was born in the same area of Rouen around 1612. She died in October 1688 at 76.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Baptiste Godefroy and Marie LeNeuf had nine sons and two daughters. Their first child, Michel born in October 1637 is considered to be the first white child born in Trois-Rivières. The second son, Louis born in 1639, was a pr
2 CONC ocurer for the King of France. Third son, Jacques de Vieuxpont, born in 1641, was a renown interpreter and fur merchant. In 1661, he left on a fur-trading mission with another Frenchman and some thirty Indians. On the way, they we
2 CONC re attacked by of band of about eighty Iroquois who they resisted for more than two days. In the end, he fell to the Iroquois gunfire. Only one of his companions, an Indian, survived the attack. The Iroquois lost about a third of 
2 CONC their party.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter, Jeanne born in 1644 joined the Ursulines in Quebec. The second daughter, Marie-Reine (Renée) born in 1652 and married to Pierre BOULANGER in 1677 is my direct ancestor.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www2.csduroy.qc.ca/DB-ELS/projets_eleves/tonnancour_2001/JeanGodefroy.htm>; a translated version follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT JEAN GODEFROY de LINTOT
2 CONT and
2 CONT MARIE LENEUF de la POTHERIE
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the nine interpreters that probably used Champlain for their contact with the Amerindian, there was Jean Godefroy.  This one arrived at the new world with his brother Thomas, of Rouen, in Normandie, in 1626.  He had then 18 
2 CONC years.  they had as parents  Pierre Godefroy and Perette Cavelier and they were born in 1607 or 1608.  When Quebec was taken by the brothers Kirke, in 1629, he lived with the Huron wendats and the Algonquiens and learned their lan
2 CONC guages with a facilitated surprise.  As soon as Champlain returned, he established himself at Three Rivers as his cousin Jean Paul.  Jean is better known in the history under the names of Lintôt and of Normanville, according to tw
2 CONC o small towns, in the department of the Seine-Inférieure.  (Another document on him does mention that he is a native of the Trinité-of the-Mount, close to Lillebonne, in the Havre-actuel district, department of the Seine-Maritime)
2 CONC .  He married in December 1636 with Marie Leneuf at the age of 29 years.  Unfortunately the marriage certificate remained untraceable, but the marriage contract is dated back to December 15 1636.  This one came from Caen, also sit
2 CONC uated in Normandie and she was the daughter of Mathieu Leneuf and Jeanne Marchand.  She was born between 1611 and 1616.  This June 11 1636, she arrived in the strengthened city of Quebec in company of her mother, that was widowed,
2 CONC  of her two brothers Michel and Jacques, and of her two nieces Anne and Marie having all the two four years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1638, the company of the Associated Cents he conceeded the seigneurie of Godefroy (about 200 acres) in the region of Trifluvienne.  He was one of the first founders of Three Rivers in 1634.  He decided to become a pioneer and c
2 CONC olonizing.  He was elected a member of the Quebec Counsel in 1648 by the three syndics of Three Rivers.  This same year, Hélie Grimard does baptize one of his sons, John.  The godfather is John Godefroy, the godmother, Marie Margu
2 CONC erie.  It is important to mention that he is the first father of a white child in the region of Three Rivers.  This is when he decided it was the right time to baptize Michel, a fine future warrior, that John was mentioned in the 
2 CONC registers of Three Rivers October 21 1637.  On his side, Marie Leneuf had established herself in this city in 1636, some months only after her arrival in New France.  The first mention of her name dates back to December 12 of this
2 CONC  same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The eleven children of this couple born between the month of October 1637 and the month of April 1658, with an average of a child every two years, (to do a lien) like the majority of the women of New France to this era.  Among the
2 CONC se children, one died at birth, another is probably dead at the age of ten years and a last one was killed by the Iroquois towards the age of 20 years.  All the others attained and surpassed the majority, that was of 25 years.  At
2 CONC  the time of the birth of his first baby, Marie Leneuf of Potherie was elderly between 21 and 26, and to the birth of her eleventh child, she had to be elderly between 42 and 47 years, nothing is more normal for the pioneer of thi
2 CONC s French colony.  The manuscripts show that Marie Leneuf was a wise woman.  While John Godefroy was ennobled by the king Louis XIV in 1668, while receiving nobility letters for him as well as for his brothers and his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, he acquired another seigneurie on the shore south of Saint Laurent, just opposite of Three Rivers.  He was granted the first November 1637 and he took the name of Lintôt and Godefroy.  He had as a border to the west the
2 CONC  river Godefroy and increased by the Isle Marie, situated on this same river, January 16 1657.  (Today this is the parish of Saint-Gregoire that is there located, at the same time the famous Inn Godefroy.)  Despite these two gigan
2 CONC tic situated lands on the shore south of Three Rivers, Marie and Jean lived inside the fort of Three Rivers, possibly on the very current site of the Manor of Tonancourt, a batch that they possessed since 1661.  Since the seigneur
2 CONC ies did not begin developing actually before 1665, because of the constant threat of the Iroquois, they did not live then on their south seigneurie.  To many returns, one attempted a beginning of defringement on the island in 1663
2 CONC , but it was quickly stopped.  The first establishments in the adjacent seigneuries like the one of Godefroy date back to the end of the XVIIe century.  In 1723, he there had not only the two granted batches on the seigneurie of L
2 CONC intôt and on the island Marie, but without a house.
2 CONT
2 CONT The brave John Godefroy, sieur of Lintôt, was buried at Three Rivers March 26 1678 at about 70 years.  (Another document does states that he is not dead until July 1681) Marie Leneuf, his wife, survived following his death by abou
2 CONC t ten years and died October 27 1688.  She was buried next to her husband the next day while she approached 76 years.  Their descendents are numerous strong in all North America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliographie:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://station05.qc.ca/Partenaires/SPHQ/Dossier/Trois-RivA·res.html The manoir of Tonnancourt, collection
