1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/d/e/s/Thelma-Deschamps-/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-0236.html  de la Ronde LETTERS - Ida Schneider to J. C. Bonenfant From the Nipigon Historical Museum Archives
2 CONT
2 CONT March 24, 1964
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. J.C. Bonenfant Bibliothecaire Hotel du Gouvernement Quebec City Canada
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Mr. Bonenfant,
2 CONT
2 CONT I take the liberty to address this letter to you in regard to some historical enquiries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Introducing myself, I like to inform you that I came to Canada in 1927, originally from Austria and Italy and - outside of the nine years residence in Montreal - I have been living in Toronto.  For the past 20 years I have been re
2 CONC ading a great deal on Canadian History and, especially in connection with the Fur Trade in the Upper Lakes.  I came across some interesting historic material, as yet unpublished.
2 CONT
2 CONT It concerns the life of two Fur Traders, namely, Louis Denys de la Ronde and Charles Francois Denys de la Ronde, Count of Thibaudiere.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their grandfather was Louis Denys de la Ronde a Chevalier de Saint-Louis, who built the first vessel in Lake Superior (1733 ) and who died in Quebec City in 1741.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their father ( one of the sons of the above named Louis was Pierre Francois Paul, born 14 juillot 1723 at l'Ile-St. Jean, died at La Collo under Abercrombie. He was married to Marguerite Susanne de Celles-Duclos in 1749 in Montrea
2 CONC l.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Denys de la Ronde, the Fur Trader, ( and son of Pierre Francois Paul ) made a Will in Montreal in 1797 in which he left his worldly possessions to an Indian wife ( common law) in the Lake Nipissing District, Ontario. He had 
2 CONC 10 children with her and in the Will he named the 10 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Louis Denys de la Ronde must have recovered his health because in July 1800 he married in Montreal a widow, her name was Louise King and with whom he had five sons.  He died in 1808 and was buried in St. Anne de Bellevue.
2 CONC  The five sons were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis, born 1801 ( of Nipigon) Charles-Francois ( named after the uncle) born 1803 Michel-Gaspard, born 1804 Pierre-Alexandre, born 1806 and Joseph-Adolphe, born 1808
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Denys de la Ronde owed his brother Charles- Francois with whom he also dealt with in the Fur Trade, a total of  L 11,355.10, this includes a loan of  L 9031 .  A signed statement for this amount against the estate of Louis i
2 CONC s in the Courthouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT The said Louise King, Widow, was appointed tutor and Charles-Francois (1763-1840) the uncle, was present in Montreal to the deed.  The one son of Louis and Louise King who is of special interest in this research is Michel-Gaspard,
2 CONC  born in 1804, who became a notary and practiced law in Avonteuil (not sure of spelling as the copy is blurry - ed). His life is described in the following two books:
2 CONT
2 CONT a) "History of the Counties of Argenteuil, Quebec, and Prescott, Ont." ( from the earliest settlement to the present) by C. Thomas 1896, Montreal.
2 CONT
2 CONT b) "Memories of Old St. Andrews and Historical Sketches of the Seigniory of Argenteuil" , by B. N. Wales, M.D.  copyright 1934 Watchman Press, Lachute, P.Q.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the above two books, Michel Gaspard de Coligny Denys de la Ronde is described as a highly intelligent lawyer, well known for his witty political lampoons etc. etc.  He was a friend of Laurier.  Michel Gaspard married in 1829 an
2 CONC d had 10 children, he died age 78 in 1896.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Francois Denys de la Ronde.  He was born June 9, 1763, in what was then known as l'Assumption du Detroit ( now Sandwich, Windsor)  his father being then an officer in that Fort.  He too became a free Fur Trader in the Uppe
2 CONC r Lakes, perhaps, at first, with the North-West Company.  He also had a family of several children with an Indian woman.  However, he brought his family to Montreal, and on the 22nd of June 1818 he had his marriage and children le
2 CONC gitimized, baptized, in OKA,  Parish of Lac des Deux Moutagnes, District Terrebonne.  He died in 1840 in Penetanguishene and his descendants  are still living and fairly numerous.
2 CONT
2 CONT About thirty years ago a direct descendant of this family of Charles Francois came from the USA to Montreal and had a visit with Mr. Massicotte.  He told her about that lawyer de la Ronde in St. Andrews East, that he had carried o
2 CONC n a long litigation with the Sulpician Order  in regard to some family property, but that , in the end he lost out on account of lack of proper evidence. Mr. Massicotte also said that the case was well known in Montreal and, in ca
2 CONC se the visitor was interested, his son-in-law, a lawyer, would be willing to look into the matter.
2 CONT
2 CONT A year or two later, a member of the same family made a trip to St. Andrews East and there met Eva de la Ronde, then about 70 years old and daughter of the late notary Michel-Gaspard.  She too confirmed the above story.
2 CONT
2 CONT A month ago I came across in the Toronto Reference Library three booklets with the following titles:
2 CONT
2 CONT A) Report, relating to the Affairs of the OKA Indians, made to the Superintendent General of Indian Affairs by Rev. William Scott,  Ottawa, 22 Jan. 1883, printed by MacLean, Roger & Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT B) A Contribution to a proper understanding of the OKA question and a help to its equitable settlement by BETA, Montreal 1879
2 CONT
2 CONT C) An historical notice on the difficulties arisen between the Seigneurs of Saint Sulpice and certain Indians of OKA , Montreal.
2 CONT
2 CONT The interesting part in these 3 booklets is that it mentions that third and fourth influential parties were using the simple Indians in this litigation.  However, no name is quoted who these parties were?
2 CONT
2 CONT My question now is : Does the above refer to Gaspard de Coligny Denys de la Ronde, it looks as if this is the same case?
2 CONT
2 CONT It seems, as if Charles Francois Denys de la Ronde also enters in this picture, because he left POWER OF ATTORNEY  to the Hon. Michel Chartier de Lotbiniere on August 13th , 1808 before Notary Public in Montreal. With this documen
2 CONC t Charles Francois claims his rights of succession to the Denys estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT My question is
2 CONT
2 CONT a) Where can I read about this succession and
2 CONT
2 CONT b) Exactly, what was the case Gaspard de Coligny de la Ronde against the Sulpicians Mr. Massicotte spoke of?
2 CONT
2 CONT A year or so ago, there appeared an article in the Daily Paper again referring to the properties the Sulpicians are holding.  Is this the same case?
2 CONT
2 CONT It may also be of interest to you in regard to the Ontario Denys de la Ronde's that an article appeared in the Toronto Telegram of December 28, 1950, stating the case of 3 Indians in the Nipigon Region.  They were descendants of t
2 CONC he following :
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Denys de la Ronde, born in St. Ann's (?).... 1803
2 CONT
2 CONT joined the North-West Company in ...1818 Joined the Hudson's Bay Company in...1821 Served at the Nipigon Post ...1851--68
2 CONT
2 CONT died at the Nipigon Post ... August 1868
2 CONT
2 CONT Although there seems to be  an error in his birth year ( the above information was given from the Hudson's Bay Records in Winnipeg ) I believe the above Louis Denys de la Ronde was the son of Louise King and Louis Denys, who died 
2 CONC in St. Anne de Bellevue in 1808. The Louis was buried at Sault Ste. Marie and in the Historical Room in the new Armories of that city there is a broken tombstone with the following inscription :
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sacred to the memory of Louis Denys de la Ronde departed this life August 22, 1868, aged 68 years.  He was for a period of 18 years in the service of the Hon. Hudson's Bay Company and was one of their most zealous and faithful of
2 CONC ficers."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Louis of the Sault left seven legitimate children ( he had married an Indian woman ) and appointed 3 trustees in his Will.  The half-Indian descendants showed that this estate had been turned over to the third trustee, a certa
2 CONC in Mr. Colin Rankin in 1881, but the Half-Indians never got the money.  The article states that the Lakehead lawyer (?) in Fort William - Port Arthur investigated these claims for two years. How it ended I don't know.  These desce
2 CONC ndants had French documents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before I close this letter, I would like to state that I have been conducting this research on and off as a hobby, I have no personal financial gains from it, I was simply fascinated by the story, peopled with French Nobility, Fur
2 CONC  Traders, Indians, Sulpicians and a host of descendants. The history of the Denys de la Ronde family has never been written, some of the descendants went even far into Western Canada with exploratory expeditions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prof. Clayton Gray, who teaches history at the George Williams College in Montreal, read some of the material I have gathered.  He thinks I have enough for a most interesting book on never before published Canadiana.  This should 
2 CONC really be written by a French -Canadian, perhaps under a Canadian Council Grant!!
2 CONT
2 CONT If you would be kind enough to answer my question contained in this letter, please wrsite in the French Language which I read very well but cannot claim the same for my own French writing.  Italian and German were my own bilingual
2 CONC  languages and I have retained them to the full. Please excuse that my letter is in English.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hoping to hear from you, I remain
2 CONT
2 CONT Sincerely yours (Mrs) Ida Schneider.
2 CONT
2 CONT Posted by B. Brill, Curator at 11:01 Email ThisBlogThis!Share to TwitterShare to FacebookShare to Pinterest 2 comments:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gilbert Deschamps21 March 2014 19:11
2 CONT
2 CONT     My father, Paul W. Deschamps, was the great-grandson of Louis Denys Delaronde through his daughter Mrs. Pierre Deschamps (nee Louise Delaronde).
2 CONT     Sincerely,
2 CONT     Gilbert Deschamps (Barrister & Solicitor)
