1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Baptiste_Richardville Wikipedia]:=
2 CONT Jean Baptiste de Richardville (c. 1761 – 13 August 1841), known as Pinšiwa in Miami (meaning Wildcat, also spelled Peshewa) and Joseph Richardville, was the last akima (civil chief) of the Miami people. He was a signatory to the T
2 CONC reaty of Greenville (1795) and later treaties with the United States through the Treaty of Mississinwas (1826). A fur trader who controlled an important portage connecting the Maumee River to the Little River, by his death in 1841
2 CONC  he was considered the wealthiest man in Indiana. He had acquired more than 20 square miles of property along the rivers.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1827 he completed construction of a treaty house partially funded by the United States. The Richardville House is the first in northeastern Indiana to be built in the Greek Revival style, the oldest Native American house in the
2 CONC  state, and one of the few surviving treaty houses in the United States. It was designated in 2012 as a National Historic Landmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT Jean Baptiste de Richardville was born about 1761 in the Miami (Myaami) village of Kekionga (present-day Fort Wayne, Indiana), son of Tacumwah, sister of the Miami chief Pacanne, and Joseph Drouet de Richerville, a French fur trad
2 CONC er from Quebec. The boy was well educated, and learned to speak Miami, an Algonquian language; Iroquois, French, and English. He gained his status in the tribe from his mother's people, as it had a matrilineal system. As an adult,
2 CONC  Peshewa later refused to speak white/European languages or wear European-style clothing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richardville and his mother made most of their income from the fur trade and control of a portage connecting the Maumee River to the Little River, known today as the Little Wabash River. The Northwest Ordinance of the United State
2 CONC s Congress declared that all important portages in the region were to be free for use. The Miami lost control of the portage in the Treaty of Greenville (1795), which ended the Northwest Wars by which a coalition of Native America
2 CONC ns had tried to repulse the European Americans from the region west of the Appalachians and north of the Ohio River.
2 CONT
2 CONT But, Richardville acquired a trade license in 1815 which gave him a monopoly on carry-over services at the portage. He earned considerable profits again from trading traffic at the portage.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1818, Richardville signed the Treaty of St. Mary's with the US. The treaty punished the Miami for their lack of support to the US during its War of 1812 against the British, and forced them to cede most of their land in central
2 CONC  Indiana to the United States.[4] At the time, Richardville negotiated legal land grants in the area to individual Miami families, including for himself. He eventually controlled more than 20 square miles along the St. Joseph, St.
2 CONC  Mary's, Mississinewa, Salamonie and Wabash rivers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pinšiwa often offered his private lands as a refuge for other Miami. This enabled about half of the Miami people to remain in Indiana when the tribe was officially removed by the US in 1846 to Indian Territory west of the Mississi
2 CONC ppi River, five years after Richardville died.
2 CONT
2 CONT After signing the Treaty of Mississinwas (1826), Richardville was given $600 by the United States toward building the Richardville House in present-day Fort Wayne, where he lived the rest of his life. Richardville used his own fun
2 CONC ds for the remainder of the total estimated cost at the time of $2200 to build the house; when completed in 1827, it was the first Greek Revival-style house in northeastern Indiana.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of his death in 1841, Richardville is considered to have been the richest man in the state of Indiana. He was succeeded as chief by his eldest daughter's husband, Francis La Fontaine. The Richardville House remained in
2 CONC  the family until 1894. It was then acquired by a gravel company that mined all but about an acre surrounding the house. The structure was designated a National Historic Landmark in 2012, as it is one of the few surviving treaty h
2 CONC ouses and the oldest Native American house in the Midwest.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1991, the house was acquired by the Allen County-Fort Wayne Historical Society. It has restored the exterior, opens the house monthly during the summer season for visitors, and operates a variety of programs on Miami history at
2 CONC  the site.
2 CONT
2 CONT =From [http://www.geni.com/people/Scott-Richardville-Ridge/6000000031518468804?through=6000000032403482589 Scott Richardville-Ridge]:=
2 CONT Jean Baptiste Richardville is a distant relative of mine (2nd cousin 5 x removed). I say distant in reference to the fact that  I was born over 120 years after his passing.  His Great Grandfather and my 6th Great Grandfather are t
2 CONC he same person: Claude Drouet de' Richerville (1665-1748). Claude was the first of our Drouet de' Richerville/ Richardville ancestors to arrive in New France from France. Jean Baptiste seemed to be quite a good negotiator, accordi
2 CONC ng to the research I've discovered. He carried considerable influence among the Miami tribe, his people. He was reportedly made Great Chief of the Miami tribe following a daring rescue of a white man from burning at the stake. The
2 CONC re is a plaque commemorating this event, which is pictured in JBRs media file. He managed to stave off the removal of the Miami from the Fort Wayne area for decades! I'm very proud to be related to Jean Baptiste Richardville "Last
2 CONC  Great Chief of the Miami Indian Tribe"
