1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT "THE STATE REGISTER" newspaper, 8 March 1879 - have copy of newspaper clipping John T. De Laronde is dead. On Sunday last at half past two o`clock pm. March 2, 1879, at his residence in the town of Caledonia, died John T. De Laron
2 CONC de, who was the oldest settler of this city, except the two children of Pierre Pauquette, on of whom Moses, was born in 1828, the year in which Mr. Laronde arrived. John T. De Laronde was born at Bordeaux in France Feb. 25, 1802. 
2 CONC From a personal narrative written by him and published in the Wisconsin Historical Collection it appears that his ancestors were men of note in the military and marine service of his native country. His father, Louis Denys Chevali
2 CONC er De Laronde was born at Detroit, Michigan while the grandfather, an officer in the French service was stationed at Detroit, previous to the final surrender of the Canadas by the French to the English in 1760. This same grandfath
2 CONC er as appears from said personal narrative and from anote appended thereto, by Hon. Lyman C. Draper, was at one time in command at Chequamegon, on Lake Superior. The father of John remained in France till the battle of Waterloo wh
2 CONC en not wishing to live under the rule of Louis XVIII he came to Canada with all his family, and became a partner in the North West Company. He died at Montreal in 1818. The son, John, received a good education both in French and E
2 CONC nglish. He seems to have been in the college of Montreal at a very early age, and to have studied medicine under Dr. Robert Nelson V. Smith, but never practiced it as a profession. In 1819, he became a clerk of the North West Comp
2 CONC any, with which company he remained seven years, during which time he went to London, England as a witness in a dispute between his company and the Hudsons Bay Company. A settlement having been effected between said companies, he 
2 CONC was transferred to the Hudsons Bay Company with which company he remained till 1828, at which time he came to Fort Winnebage, now Portage. This was the year in which old Fort Winnebago was built. Her Mr. Laronde engaged as clerk o
2 CONC f the American Fur Co. under the noted Pierre Pauquette, who was then, and for some time thereafter, agent of that company. Her Mr. LaRonde married an Indian woman of the DeKaury tribe of Winnebagoes.
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
