1 NAME Antoine /Girardin/
2 GIVN Antoine
2 SURN Girardin
2 _MARNM Girardin
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 9 SEP 1808
2 ADDR
3 CITY Maskinongé
3 STAE Quebec
3 CTRY Canada
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 JUN 1891
2 ADDR
3 CITY West Boylston
3 STAE Massachusetts
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In 1869, Antoine Girardin (age 64) and family—wife Luce Alary, Joseph (age 15) , Alfred (age 18) , Honore (Henry) (age 23), Amanuel(age 14),  brother Casimir (age 46)---moved from St Felix de Kinsey in Quebec to W.
2 CONC  Boylston, Mass. According to the 1870 census, Antoine  Sr, had no occupation and all children worked in the Mill. They had a servant named Ann Gately. Casimir worked on a farm.  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 1880 census, Joseph,  Amanuel, and Henry were  bootmakers .Both Antoine Sr and Jr were carpenters. . Another of Antoine’s  son, Antoine Jr. moved from Quebec in 1878 at age 38 with his wife, Mary Alice and son Jos
2 CONC eph (age 11) to W. Boylston . His wife Marie Alice died in June 19 , 1894 (age Antoine Sr died in W. Boylston at age 87--  ). According to the 1900 Hudson census, he remarried in 1895 to Jennie. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph , Antoine Sr.’s son, had married Louisa Collier in 1877. She was from Holden Mass.  . As of 1879, he was living in W. Boylston. In a Worcester Probate Court record in 1883, he said he was then living in Holden Mass.. His wi
2 CONC fe Louisa (Collier) died about 1887-1889. Her father, Isaac Collier, died in 1929. In his obit, it stated that she died more than 40 years before. I have not found any trace of her death. When I have some free time, I may look thr
2 CONC ough the Worcester Evening Gazette microfiche of that time period for evidence.  I know from my Uncle Harold and father that their 4 kids were placed in foster homes. He had moved to Minnesota about 1900. He returned when my fathe
2 CONC r was young (age 10-12 ~ 1932) and visited for a short time. According to the 1910 Minnesota Census, he was living with his sister –Luce Marie(59)-and brother-in-law, Charles Beauchesne (64).  He was still a shoemaker –AGE 55). 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 1920 Minn census, he was remarried to Mary and working in a mill. By 1930, according to the census, he was single again and living as a boarder with a man named  Armenia Ledoux and Ledoux’s daughter. Joseph died S
2 CONC ept 25, 1945 in Minnesota.(see death cert below)
2 CONT
2 CONT Amanuel moved about 1898 to Leominster, Mass. Where, according to the 1900 census, he was living and working as a buffer . He died there in Dec 27 1904. Henry , 1900, according to the census was still living in W. Boylston, but mo
2 CONC ved to Webster, Ma. , was living in Webster according to 1910 Census and died Sept 24,1919 there . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfred, in 1900 census, he had moved to Northbridge, ma. Where he was a wood chopper. He is also listed in the 1910 Northbridge census. But in 1910 , he moved to Petersboro, NH with his wife, where he and wife are listed in the 19
2 CONC 20 census. He must have died a few years after, since he is not in the 1930 census.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine Jr moved to Hudson in 1897. His son Joseph O. had moved there in 1895. He was also a carpenter.  In the 1900 Hudson Census, he is listed as a carpenter and remarried to Jennie. Not in 1910 census. Don’t know when he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to my research in Worcester, Ma. Probate Court, in 1870 and 1871, Henry and Alfred Girardin, 2 brothers, bought land from Peter Leroux And Peter Martin. In 1872, Antoine, another brother, bought Henry’s land.  In 1877, a
2 CONC  4th brother , Joseph, bought Antoine’s land for $400 on an  8 year mortgage.  On May 3rd 1881, on an affidavid, Joseph admits that he is in default on the Mortgage, giving  Antoine the  right to auction off property. He puts it i
2 CONC n the daily paper, the Worcester Spy for 4 weeks and his wife Mary Alice buys it for $100. On Feb 16, 1883, Joseph says that his brother  Antoine illegally forged his signature on the May 3rd 1881 affidavid that said he was in def
2 CONC ault when he wasn't. Joseph  finally got paid back and released the mortgage and land to Antoine.  In another series of transactions, in Aug 28, 1878, Antoine buys Alfred’s land for $1.00  . That same day, Eliza (Lapointe) Girardi
2 CONC n, Alfred’s wife buys the land back for $1.00 . Less than a month later, on Oct 19th, 1878, Antoine buys it back for $1.00 .  In another series of transactions, in Sept 28 1878, Mary Alice , Antoine Jr's wife,   buys land from Joe
2 CONC l Walker.  On Sept 20 1882, Antoine Jr  buys land from Joel Walker. Finally , on July 22 1884, Antoine Jr  buys more land from Joel Walker. All 3 are mortgage deals.  On June 1894, Mary Alice dies, putting all of the land in Antoi
2 CONC ne’s hands.   In 1895, Mass. Decides to create the Wachusetts reservoir, meaning that allot of people will loose their homes and businesses. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On Dec 26 1896, Antoine jr pays off all mortgages to Joel Walker, and  Antoine Jr Owns land. Two days later, he sells the land to the state for $1.00   . There are other land deals involving Antoine the  father. All of this wheeli
2 CONC ng and dealing is rather confusing and I can understand that Joseph would be rather upset with his brother Antoine over the forgery deal. May be that is why he leaves WB and moves to Holden  , where his Father in law Isaac Collier
2 CONC  lives.  More tragedy happens a few years later in 1887-1889 when his wife dies.  For whatever reason, he gives up his kids, ages 5-10 to a foster home and moves to Minnesota. He died in Minn.  After they are forced to sell their 
2 CONC houses, all of the brothers decide to separate.  Antoine Jr goes to Hudson, Mass..in 1897 . Joseph O, Antoine Jr’s son continues his father’s occupation as a carpenter in Hudson. He married Julia  Brunault in 1891 and they had 5 s
2 CONC ons—Antoine J,  Henry C  (m. Rose Alma LeBlanc 1917),  Alphonse J,  Henry E, and Walter. Joseph O’s  obituary on Feb 4th 1952 is below.  Joseph O’s sons, Antoine J. and Henry C  , also were carpenters in Hudson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfred moves to Northbridge, Mass and later to Petersboro, NH.  He and wife Elise Laporte and 4 sons and 6 daughters.. Henry moves to Webster, Ma.  He and wife Ermine Bergerant, had 5 sons and 3 daughters.  One son, Phillip marrie
2 CONC d Christina Gauthier in 1908.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amanuel goes to Leominster, Ma. He and wife Mary Peloquin (m 1878)  had 6 sons and 1 daughter.  Hudson and Leominster and fairly close. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine Sr’s  brother, Casimir,  had died in 1882 in WB. With his wife, Emelie Lesieur, they had 17 kids, all born in southern QUE.  Two of his sons, Albert S. and Joseph D. had moved to the Worcester area. According to the Leomin
2 CONC ster City Directory, Albert S. was living in Leominster in 1890 and was a dress maker and cutter.  He died there Nov 1 1924  . Albert S married Emma Francour in 1881 and had 2 sons—Arthur A and Ernest L.  Albert S moved to Leomins
2 CONC ter in 1889.  Arthur A married Emma Petherick in 1913 and died in Leominster Nov 11 1963. (see Obit below) .  Adrien D (m. Julie Asselyn in 1867) moved to Worcester in 1868 and died there in Sept 28 1910. He was a dry goods clerk,
2 CONC  a liquor dealer, and ran a pool room. 
2 CONT
