1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Josette (La Framboise) Pierce'''
2 CONT Born 24 Sep 1795 in Michilimackinac, Michigan, United States 
2 CONT Died 24 Nov 1820 at age 25 in Mackinac Island, Mackinac, Michigan, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Josette "Josephine" Pierce formerly La Framboise
2 CONT '''Daughter''' of Louis Joseph Laframboise and Marguerite-Magdelaine (Marcot) La Framboise
2 CONT '''Sister''' of Joseph Laframboise
2 CONT '''Wife''' of Benjamin Kendrick Pierce — married 2 Apr 1816 in Mackinac Island, Mackinac, Michigan Territory, United States
2 CONT '''Mother''' of Daniel Pierce and Benjamin Langdon Pierce
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT Josette was born about 1795. She was the daughter of Joseph La Framboise, who married Marguerite-Magdelaine Marcot, daughter of Jean Baptiste Marcotte, a fur-trader of Mackinaw, who married a woman of the Ottawa tribe of Indiana. 
2 CONC After the death of her father, Madame La Framboise became one of the most successful trappers and traders of the American West. Because of her connections to the French Canadian trappers, the Indians, as well as the White soldiers
2 CONC  and settlers she became a very prominent woman. Her chief station, as agent of the American Fur Company (funded and backed by John Jacob Astor) she was wealthy enough, and wise enough, to see that her children received formal sch
2 CONC ooling and became proficient in English, French, as well as local Indian dialects.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 7,1799, the ceremonies of baptism were supplied to Josette,born on September 24,1795 to Joseph Laframboise and Madeline, of the nation of the courtes oreilles. (signed by Gabriel Richard, priest).[3] In the summer of 1817,
2 CONC  at Mackinaw, Josette, reportedly a singularly beautiful girl, became the wife of Benjamin K Pierce, brother of Franklin Pierce, the 14th President of the United States.[2] Josette and Benjamin Pierce had two children, Harriet Jos
2 CONC ephine Pierce (1818-1854) and Benjamin Langdon Pierce (1820-1820). Reportedly, Josette died due to complications following the birth of her son on November 20, 1820; with the son dying several days later. She is buried, alongside 
2 CONC her mother, on the grounds of St. Anne's Cemetery in Mackinac Island, Michigan.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources'''
2 CONT 1. ↑ American Fur Company Invoices: pg 373 of Collections of the State of Wisconsin, Vol 11
2 CONT 2. ↑ 2.0 2.1
2 CONT 3. ↑ Wikipedia page for Magdelaine Framboise[1]
2 CONT 4. ↑ Find A Grave: Memorial #21803599
2 CONT See also:
2 CONT -https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LZP9-NBC
2 CONT -Wisconsin Historical Society Data
2 CONT -Web Page Author: Brian Tompsett, Data Author: Funk&Wagnall's "The Presidents", Genealogy of the US Presidents, web page, (http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/public/genealogy/presidents/, Version: 23 Apr 96), "Electronic."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source:'''WikiTree contributors, "Josette (La Framboise) Pierce (1795-1820)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/La_Framboise-270 : accessed 25 June 2025).
2 CONT ..... 
2 CONT
2 CONT Josette Laframboise was the daughter of Joseph Laframboise, who married Madeline,daughter of Jean Baptiste Marcotte, a fur-trader of Mackinaw, who married a woman of the Ottawa tribe of Indiana.  On July 7,1799, the ceremonies of 
2 CONC baptism were supplied to Josette,born on September 24,1795 of Joseph Laframboise and Madeline,of the nation of the courtes oreilles. (signed by Gabriel Richard,priest)
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her father, Madame La Framboise became one of the most successful trappers and traders of the American West. Because of her connections to the French Canadian trappers, the Indians, as well as the White soldiers
2 CONC  and settlers she became a very prominent woman.  Her chief station, as agent of the American Fur Company (funded and backed by John Jacob Astor) she was wealthy enough, and wise enough, to see that her children received formal sc
2 CONC hooling and became proficient in English, French, as well as local Indian dialects.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1817, at Mackinaw, her daughter Josette, a singularly beautiful girl, became the wife of Benjamin K Pierce, brother of Franklin Pierce.
2 CONT *Wisconsin Historical Society Data
2 CONT *American Fur Company Invoices: pg 373 of Collections of the State of Wisconsin, Vol 11
2 CONT
2 CONT Children were: Harriet and Langdon.
2 CONT
2 CONT  (Despite some sources reporting a son Daniel, there were only 2 children born of her marriage to Benjamin Kendrick Pierce...the record of a Daniel implies that Daniel was left in the care of his grandmother Madame LaFramboise, at
2 CONC  Ft. Mackinac when he was re-assigned to Florida...but this is ridiculous, since Benjamin's sister Elizabeth and her husband were at Ft. Mackinac, and records show that they cared for daughter Harriet for a good nuber of years...a
2 CONC nd that the only other offspring died shortly after birth...son Langdon)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 1820 census Michilimackinack County,Michigan shows two adults (1 male, 1 female, 1 male child under 10, 2 females under 10) ......... Colonel Pierce's personal life was to a considerable extent one of tragedy and anxiety. He
2 CONC  was married three times,each of his wives dying after a few years of marraige. His first marriage was in 1816 to Josephine LaFramboise, of Mackinac Island,Michigan,daughter of wealthy and prominent fur-traders. He was 26, she was
2 CONC  21. Two children, a daughter and son, were born to them. She died in childbirth in November of 1820, at the age of 25, and the son died a few days later.
2 CONT
2 CONT *St Lucie Historical Society (from articles submitted to the News Tribune by Editor Emeritus, Charles S Miley)
2 CONT *Info from St Lucie County Historical Society and Massachusetts NEHGS
2 CONT *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdelaine_Laframboise
