1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Joseph Laframboise'''
2 CONT Born 1805 in Mackinac Island, Mackinac, Michigan, United States 
2 CONT Died 9 Nov 1856 at about age 51 in Nicollet, Minnesota, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Joseph Laframboise'''
2 CONT '''Son''' of Louis Joseph Laframboise and Marguerite-Magdelaine (Marcot) La Framboise
2 CONT '''Brother''' of Josette (La Framboise) Pierce
2 CONT '''Husband''' of Oasixheaoui (Lakota) Laframboise — married 1838 in Minnesota map icon Husband of Jane (Dickson) Sharron — married 1845 [location unknown]
2 CONT '''Father''' of Joseph Edouard Laframboise, Alexis George Laframboise, Julia Ann Laframboise, William Robert Laframboise and Justine Madeline Laframboise
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT Joseph was born at the Grand River Post, the son of Madeleine Marcotte and Joseph Laframboise. His mother is famous as the first businesswoman of Michigan and a very prosperous fur trader. Madeleine had taken control of the family
2 CONC  business after her husband was murdered by White Ox for, a Potawatomi, for refusing to give him liquor. His murder happened in the fall of 1806 on their annual trip to Grand River Joseph’s entourage of voyageurs were camped on th
2 CONC e beach of Lake Michigan about a days travel from the mouth of the Grand River. Madeleine was thus left a widow at age 24. she carried on and went ahead with that season’s trading. She sold the business to the American Fur Company
2 CONC  (AFC) in 1821. John Jacob Astor, the owner of AFC became Joseph Jr’s first employer in 1826. As an agent for the company he opened the Great Oasis, Murray County Post in the early 1830s. He worked in a number of posts as far west
2 CONC  as Watertown, South Dakota.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph was married first to a Mdewakanton woman. His next two wives were Sisseton Sioux; daughters of Chief Sleepy Eye. He married these sisters, Oasixheaoui and Magdaleine, in 1838. "Oasixheaoui had three children before she died
2 CONC . Joseph LaFramboise Jr. was born in 1831; Alexis LaFramboise, in 1840 and Julia, in 1842. Oasixheaoui died by the end of 1844 when Julia was about two years old. Joseph Sr. then married Jane Dickson, the daughter of fur trader Ro
2 CONC bert Dickson, in 1845. Jane was Mdewakanton, Ojibwe and Scots and was the only mother that Alexis and Julia ever knew."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph worked in southwestern Minnesota and eastern South Dakota from the early 1820s until his death in 1856. LaFramboise was a confidant of well-known figures of the time including Henry H. Sibley, George Catlin, Joseph N. Nicol
2 CONC let, and John C. Fremont.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1837, he was the interpreter for the Sioux and Chippewa Treaty negotiations. He also interpreted in 1851 for the Traverse des Sioux Treaty. LaFramboise was a third generation Metis who became a catalyst for the removal of the S
2 CONC antee Sioux from their land to facilitate white settlement. He engaged in the first commerce, became an agent for the further acculturation of the Santee Indians, helped establish overland routes, and guided those who mapped the r
2 CONC egion for subsequent settlers.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Men of Nicollet Rock History'''
2 CONT Joseph LaFramboise was born in 1805 on Michigan's Mackinac Island to Louis Joseph LaFramboise (a French fur trader) and Marguerite-Magdelaine Marcot LaFramboise (granddaughter of Odawa Chief Kewinaquot).
2 CONT
2 CONT He followed his parents into fur trading on Dakota lands in Minnesota and married at least one - possibly two - of Sisseton Dakota Chief Sleepy Eye's daughters (notably Magdeleine "Sleepy Eyes").
2 CONT
2 CONT LaFramboise served as an interpreter for the Dakota at two treaty signings with the U.S. government.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also knew the pipestone quarries well and hosted the artist George Catlin for 2 days on his expedition through the prairies. Catlin referred to LaFramboise as a "good friend" and "jolly companionable man" who had a "great relis
2 CONC h for songs and stories, of which he gives us many."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1838, Joseph Nicollet employed LaFramboise as a guide and interpreter for his expedition. Nicollet wrote that at the pipestone quarries, LaFramboise was "tranquilly engraving his name" on a rock when a man from the Wahpekute Tr
2 CONC ibe approached him. It turned out the two men knew each other and the Wahpekute man was there with three other families to quarry. The three families camped with the expedition during their stay, sharing stories and quarrying toge
2 CONC ther.
2 CONT
2 CONT When LaFramboise's Sisseton wife died, he remarried Jane Dickson (Ojibwe and Mdewakanton) and established the Little Rock Trading Post. It ended up being near Fort Ridgely and the town of New Ulm, MN, providing lucrative trade opp
2 CONC ortunities.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000219573922842&size=large}}
2 CONT When LaFramboise died in 1856, he was the largest land owner in Nicollet County. He was buried just outside of his trading post.
2 CONT
