1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Son of Francois La Framboise and Shaw-we-no-quah (meaning south woman, also called Madaline), a Potawatomi Indian.  Became a Potawatomi chief, reportedly by marriage to Therese Peltier according to Early Chicago.co
2 CONC m.  (However, she was reportedly 1/2 French, 1/4 Creek, 1/4 Chippewa.)  He and his father were among the first voters in Chicago.  He was granted land in the Ottawa Treaty of 1821.  He received 1/4 share of Francois Laframboise es
2 CONC tate on July 13, 1831.   He received $441 in the Potawatomi Treaty of 1832.  His wife, Therese, was granted one section of land at Skunk Grove.   He received an annuity of $200 per year for life, as well as a section of land in th
2 CONC e Chicago Treaty (also known as the Chippewa Treaty).  Another record shows grants of $4200 (see the Chippewa Treaty of 1833).  He was reported to have two wives, but this is unclear.   He is listed on the 1863 Potawatomi Kansas A
2 CONC llotment Roll #668.  Information found on the Gentner Family Tree site, as well as Early Chicago Home.
2 CONT
