1 OCCU prêtre, missionnaire, auteur
1 TITL Rev.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George-Antoine Belcourt (1803-1874) was a [https://www.geni.com/projects/Jesuits/14304 Jesuit] [https://www.geni.com/projects/Missionaries/9741 missionary], priest and [https://www.geni.com/projects/Auteurs-canadie
2 CONC ns/39117 author]. He was born in Baie-du-Febvre, Quebec and ordained in 1827. He was bilingual French and English.
2 CONT * Father Belcourt aspired to do missionary work. In 1830 he went to Manitoba with Archbishop [http://www.geni.com/people/The-Most-Reverend-Bernard-Claude-Panet/6000000012148615924 Bernard-Claude Panet]. He learned [http://en.wikip
2 CONC edia.org/wiki/Anishinaabe_language Anishinaabe], the language of the Ojibwe people, in order to convert them to christianity. His book of grammar, ''Principes de la langue des sauvages appelés Sauteux'' was published in Quebec in 
2 CONC 1838.
2 CONT * He started the first native-only mission west of Saint Boniface in Manitoba, but it was closed down a year later due to raiding.
2 CONT * In 1834 he established a mission at Baie-Saint-Paul on the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assiniboine_River Assiniboine River], where he taught the local people about European-style agriculture. 
2 CONT * In 1838 he traveled to [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainy_Lake Rainy Lake] and was unsuccessful at starting a mission because the local people were unwilling to give up their Hudson's Bay Company supplied liquor, a requirement 
2 CONC for conversion to christianity.
2 CONT * In 1840 he estabished a mission among the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wabaseemoong_Independent_Nations Wabaseeemong Independent Nations]. 
2 CONT * In 1847 Beclourt believed that the First Nations people were discriminated against by the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson%27s_Bay_Company Hudson's Bay Company] in the fur trade. He prepared a petition to Queen Victoria that
2 CONC  was signed by 977 First Nations people but [http://www.geni.com/people/Gen-Sir-Charles-Grey/6000000004079698752?through=6000000003931393276 Earl Grey], the colonial secretary, took no action on the case. Moreover, Father Belcourt
2 CONC  was arrested for inciting discontent among the local people. He was quickly released and sent to [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pembina,_North_Dakota Pembina, North Dakota] as a missionary to the Chippewa.
2 CONT *Belcourt arrived at Rustico, Prince Edward Island in 1859.
2 CONT * In 1866, Belcourt was intrigued by a new form of transportation, the steam powered automobile. He purchased one from Mr. Aliza DeWare of New Jersey and had it shipped to Rustico. Belcourt had the honour to drive the first automo
2 CONC bile in Canada.
2 CONT * He retired to a farm at Shediac, New Brunswick.
2 CONT * Father Belcourt's legacy includes: Belcourt, a town in North Dakota named after the priest, and in 1959 Belcourt was designated a National Historic Person by the Canadian Government. He prepared a ''Dictionnaire sauteux'', later
2 CONC  published by Father Albert Lacombe in 1874, and he also translated the catechism of the diocese of Quebec into Chippewa.
2 CONT
