1 NAME Nelson /DEGRAFF/
2 GIVN Nelson
2 SURN DEGRAFF
1 SEX M
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Newspaper Story, South Bend Tribune Date Unknown:
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>Old Timers, Separated for Years, Get Together and Boost St. Joseph County≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>Two "old timers" a Kansan and a Hoosier, sat in a long and solemn judgment on the history of St. Joseph county's past, present and future, Sunday afternoon, here and then rendered a verdict to thiscommunity. Their decision was 
2 CONC favorable!
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>The Kansan was George Degraff, age 62, grandson of South Bend's first white settler, Pierre Navarre, and a distant kinsman of Robert LaSalle, here on a visit from Rossville, Kan. The other was his uncle, Nelson Degraff, age 81,
2 CONC  the only Degraff surviving the Degraff invasion of eight here in 1848.
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>The decision was reached in the elder Degraff's home, 729 North Hill Street, during a conference which marked the last of a series of historical discussions of the vincintiy during the last week. For George leaves to-day for Ch
2 CONC icago to visit a daughter in Riverside, Ill. - then Rossville, Kan.
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>Nelson Degraff opened the case with a recital of the Degraff family's coming here in 1848 from Burlington, Vt. and how they increased the population from 1,653 to 1,658-all in a day. Their route ashe recalled it included all th
2 CONC e towns that had railroad stations: Saratoga Springs, Rochester, Buffalo and Niles.
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>On arriving here, Nelson entered the Assumption school, now St. Joseph's Roman Catholic school, Hill Street and LaSalle Avenue. When 12 years of age, he became interested in nailing bands on grist barrels in a mill at the Colfa
2 CONC x Bridge. The civil war somehow happened along then and Nelson became a 17 year old operative in the intelligence department of the Union Army, after having previously enlisted as a private in the 30th Michigan Infantry at Niles.
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>From the war, he revealed, he returned home, to join South Bend's first volunteer fire company. The rest of his story concerned labors at the University of Notre Dame for eight years; Singers for about 16 years and the Mishawak
2 CONC a Rubber and Woolen Manufacturing company where he is now employed for 21 years. Then he let George tell it.
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>George Degraff whose chief interest in South Bend is the Navarre cabin in which he was born, took over the discussion of Rossville, Kan. connections with the St. Joseph county. Being of French extraction with "an eighth of Indi
2 CONC an blood" in his veins, he told of the Potawatomi Indians who went to Kansas and have increased from 1,500 savages to approximately 2,000 American citizens. 
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>Nelson resumed his judgement of modern conditions-but this time it was of prohibition and politics. Although pledging his support of prohibition when it is carried out, he condemned the laxity of enforcing it. Liquor for the gr
2 CONC ocery stores rather than the saloons, represented his sentiments. Then in conclusion, he denied being a "Coolidge man", applauded Al Smith as his "man", but cheered the republicans.
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>George immediately took over the case for the republicans. While refusing to comment on Coolidge, and admitting only that he regarded "good liquor" just like any other Kansan". George declared thatSenator Charles Curtis from so
2 CONC mewhere "out thar in Kansas" was his "man" but that he would become an Al Smith supporter if Kansas' favorite son went unreconized. And there the case rested--but with their verdict of St. Joseph county already recorded as favorab
2 CONC le.
2 CONT ≤p>≤/p>
2 CONT ≤p>Nelson left for work in the carpenter shop of the Mishawaka corporation this morning and George went his way to Chicago and Rossville, Kan. "away out thar in Kansas".
